WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.468
Serialization bridges program structures and
transmitted bytes.

00:00:04.468 --> 00:00:10.566
JSON and XML work well for trees, but shared
references and cycles require an explicit

00:00:10.566 --> 00:00:11.630
representation.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.963
Encode each person once and link friends by ID.

00:00:14.963 --> 00:00:21.345
Validate syntax and distinguish characters from
bytes: the ASCII example uses one byte per

00:00:21.345 --> 00:00:25.103
character, while UTF-8 supports wider characters
too.

00:00:25.103 --> 00:00:28.117
Choose technologies at the correct layer.

00:00:28.117 --> 00:00:35.248
HTTP is a protocol, JSON and Protocol Buffers
serialize data, and gRPC provides an RPC framework.

00:00:35.248 --> 00:00:38.262
REST describes architectural constraints.

00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:44.511
Reusing HTTP infrastructure is useful, but its
semantics remain part of the contract.

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:51.789
Decide using compatibility and operational
requirements, supported by measurement where
performance

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:52.377
matters.
